17592186553465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553465 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553465 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553465 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553465 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bce7582a-d7a8-568c-a03e-61260c9c5c8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553473 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d64b3832-4443-5df9-a2ae-cff83b32069c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553478 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0dcc2e6-1bc5-592f-996a-6b8e9f9cebf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553480 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "edc3d773-9201-5181-846a-f801f1bee199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553481 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f166422c-41b0-5717-a433-57648d5156df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-288441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553489 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7mx=-14x\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "021c858c-ff29-5406-946e-12cf5966af05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553489 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7m,\\,b=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7m=-14.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553493 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/63/631bae1b2aa4a34ecaa023d45b4cf0f8aebe011214deef8e23890da1a2d197d8.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09d0b1bd-784f-5b5f-a562-190332d09cbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553493 #edb/ref 1074 "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule is *not* a function because there is an input value ($x=0$) that is *not assigned* to an output value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553520 #edb/ref 1071 "Eleanor made a call to her parents, which lasted for $\\frac{1}{4}$ of an hour. For this call, she paid $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a dollar. How many dollars would she pay for a $5$ hour call?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a0694b1-4a4b-5ce3-895a-155c5691ef32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553520 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the number of dollars that Eleanor would pay for a $5$ hour call, we can first find the unit rate of dollars per hour, and then multiply the unit rate by $5$ hours.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per hour, we divide the number of dollars, $\\frac{1}{2},$ by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}÷\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 4}{2\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =4÷2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ dollars per hour. To find the number of dollars that Eleanor would pay for a $5$ hour call, we multiply the unit rate by $5$ hours and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 5 & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Eleanor would pay $10$ dollars for a $5$ hour call." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553531 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $(2y)^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "677c1e28-0032-5401-8f05-31d06a622d65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553531 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2y)^{4} & = \\\\ {2}^{4}\\cdot {y}^{4} & = \\\\ 16{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553553 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97290040-c817-56ab-b0de-389c63e1806d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553553 #edb/ref 1074 "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=11÷\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $11=\\frac{11}{1}$ and dividing by $\\frac{3}{4}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{4}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11÷\\frac{3}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{11\\times 4}{1\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{44}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{44}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553554 #edb/ref 1071 "The inequality $1\\le x\\le 5$ can be expressed in interval notation as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "974c4026-e9d5-5cd6-80d1-e15772e57911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553554 #edb/ref 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $5$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[1,5]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,5].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553555 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553555 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553555 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c368bfa8eb05583b7298e6e386ac37e36751f8de874a4822bbdcf7432e054945.png)\n\nGiven that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{LN}$ with $LM=7-x$ and $LN=11-x$, what is the value of $LN?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553555 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99403fe1-1721-56c5-b69a-61a8a55f3486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553555 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/7c/7c89598fe6a0450eddfe8af4ed274d4110fd7cb2de796bb5a749798b44c7e94a.png)\n\n  \n\nSince $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{LN}$, the segment $\\overset{―}{LN}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overset{―}{LM}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2LM & =LN \\\\ 2(7-x) & =11-x \\\\ 14-2x & =11-x \\\\ 14-2x+2x & =11-x+2x \\\\ 14 & =11+x \\\\ 14-11 & =11+x-11 \\\\ 3 & =x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  by plugging this value into the expression for $LN$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =11-x \\\\  & =11-3 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553559 #edb/ref 1071 "The perimeter $p$ of the equilateral triangle $△ABC$ is $9.$ What is the perimeter of the isosceles triangle $△ABD$ with base $\\overset{―}{AB}$ if $AD=5?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e670b5a-5fbf-5bcb-b259-10c91c8115bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553559 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's illustrate the triangles.\n\n![](images/2e/2e18009a3a454e83e42f9f21840c6821605ac2eb8f4e3d493c8468a5fe5f5acc.png)\n\n \n\nWe're told that the perimeter of the equilateral triangle $△ABC$ is ${p}_{1}=9,$ and since all $3$ sides have equal length, we get\n\n$$\nAB=\\frac{{p}_{1}}{3}=\\frac{9}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSince  $△ABD$  is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we have $AD=BD=5.$ \n\nNow, we have enough information to compute the perimeter of the triangle $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BD+AD \\\\  & =3+5+5 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553562 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0a/0a380bff517befb89bd26c88aeb014b36b4040b5d8b5ceab6c8ef7b8549b3378.png)\n\nFind the equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,1)$ and $(5,7).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abb200c5-68bb-537e-9baa-018c760a0ae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553562 #edb/ref 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-1}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+b \\\\ 1 & =2(2)+b \\\\ 1 & =4+b \\\\ 1-4 & =b \\\\ -3 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=2x-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553564 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{12}{7}=-\\frac{4}{3n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1ef5b0e-d9e2-581a-b9df-9ba5373d94d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553564 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7} & =-\\frac{4}{3n} \\\\ \\frac{12}{7} & =\\frac{-4}{3n} \\\\ 12\\cdot 3n & =7\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 36n & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36n & =-28 \\\\ n & =-\\frac{28}{36} \\\\ n & =-\\frac{28÷4}{36÷4} \\\\ n & =-\\frac{7}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $n=-\\frac{7}{9}$ is different from $n=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $n=-\\frac{7}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553577 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The sum of ${x}^{2}+x+1$ and ${x}^{2}-2x-1$ is a trinomial.\n\nII. The sum of ${x}^{2}+3x+1$ and $3{x}^{2}-x-5$ is a trinomial.\n\nIII. The sum of two trinomials is not necessarily a trinomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8eb4941-b3e2-55e9-b6fe-f87579a51eed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553577 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x+1)+({x}^{2}-2x-1)=2{x}^{2}-x,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a trinomial (it's a binomial).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3x+1)+(3{x}^{2}-x-5)=4{x}^{2}+2x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich is a trinomial.\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of two trinomials *could be* a trinomial (see, for example, statement II). \n\n However, the sum of two trinomials is *not always* a trinomial (see, for example, statement I). \n\n So, the sum of two trinomials is not necessarily a trinomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553580 #edb/ref 1071 "The formula $V(t)=100\\cdot {4}^{t}$ gives the number of times a video has been reproduced $t$ hours after it was published. How many reproductions will the video have after $6$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e11fae48-4720-5d25-bf2f-7ddb91eae65f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553580 #edb/ref 1074 "To get the number of reproductions after $6$ hours, we evaluate the function at $t=6.$ Substituting $t=6$ into the function $V(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(6) & =100\\cdot {4}^{6} \\\\  & =100\\cdot 4\\,096 \\\\  & =409\\,600.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $6$ hours, the video will have $409\\,600$ reproductions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553594 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553594 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0557292c-60c1-5105-b17d-74697485f336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553594 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a8286ab-7ac3-58b7-9062-930360691583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3738af40-25e7-5702-97e6-7c5aacf4457e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186553580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553616 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186553640 #edb/ref 17592186045